  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{2}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.$",
